Christy,

I’m sure Mike will have some additional comments, so I’ll restrict mine to one general consideration. The LDA-type functions Mike mentioned are predicated on one having a set of variables that collectively can be considered to form a multidimensional space that at least roughly follows metric properties. Without this, distances and directions have little meaning, and by extension, inferences of group membership (which are based on distances between objects) don’t either.

To ensure this property, the input variables describing the space should be in commensurate units and relative scale (Mike and I discuss this briefly in a general context in our 2019 AREES paper; with additional references therein). One should worry about combining GMM variables and other variables as input to a single analysis if the variables are not in commensurate units and scale. They may all be columns of numbers, but that does not make them equivalent, nor does it make the output useful or interpretable (read: the math might ‘work’ but has no meaning).

To do such a combination, some pre-processing would be required. One approach is to break up the variables into ‘types’, and each type is first interrogated multivariately using the appropriate distance measure. Then, scores from a PCoA are obtained separately for each data type, these are then concatenated to make a final data matrix for downstream analyses such as LDA.  An nice ecological example of this pipeline is by Pianka et al. 2017 (Am. Nat.) where they constructed multivariate ecological niches from disparate data types by using this procedure.

Hope this helps.

Dean

Dr. Dean C. Adams (he/him)

Distinguished Professor of Evolutionary Biology

Department of Ecology, Evolution, and Organismal Biology

Iowa State University

